      "origin": [7, 6],
      "author": "Hollowmere team",
      "story": "Ida Lindqvist kept the county library for thirty years and lived in the flat over its stacks. When the Cordon came for the county records and the old survey maps she told them the county's records stay in the county, so they boxed them into the basement archive instead, wired an alarm to its steel door and left a clerk down there to finish the job. She nailed the street stair shut, bolted the front doors and came and went by the fire-escape ladder to her back porch. A boy from over the laundromat turned up on his own and she read to him in the children's corner every night. At dusk the turned came back to the reading room and sat at the tables like they used to, and the boards in the stacks told her where every one of them was. The clerk was still in the archive when she locked it on him. One morning the boy was gone and the ladder was down, and Ida went to bed with a book.",
